<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western Ghats</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghat">Ghats</a> are a mountain range running parallel to the western coast of the Indian peninsula. Their rugged terrain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a> provided the base of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> power. Accounts of the Maratha Empire describe how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> used the terrain of the Western Ghats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare to seize forts such as Torna, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgad">Rajgad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raigad">Raigad</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-ghats.jpg" alt="Western Ghats" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mdmadhu · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AnaimudiPeak_DSC_4834.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Western Ghats, also known as the Sahyadri, stretch 1,600 km (990 mi) along the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and cover about 160,000 km2 (62,000 sq mi). They run through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, from the Tapti River to Swamithoppe in Kanyakumari district at the southern tip of the peninsula. They meet the Eastern Ghats in the Nilgiris. Geologists hold that they formed during the break-up of the supercontinent of Gondwana, rising along the coast in the Late Jurassic and Early Cretaceous when India separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. They are the faulted and eroded edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a>, and their average elevation is about 1,200 m (3,900 ft), with Anamudi, at 2,695 m (8,842 ft), the highest peak.</p><p>The name joins ghat, a word that can mean a range of stepped hills or the steps leading down to water, to the direction west. The linguist Thomas Burrow traced ghat to Dravidian words such as kattu, meaning mountain side, ridge or dam, in Tamil. Sahyadri, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, means benevolent or tolerant mountain. Three gaps break the range: the Goa Gap, the oldest and widest, the Palghat Gap, and the narrow Sengottai Gap in the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Historically the Western Ghats were covered in dense forest, the natural habitat of wildlife and of native tribal people. Their inaccessibility made it difficult for people from the plains to cultivate the land and build settlements. After the establishment of British colonial rule in the region, large areas were cleared for agricultural plantations and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, and the forests were severely fragmented by clear-felling. Introduced species threatened the rare endemic species, and the complex tropical rainforests suffered more than other habitats. Other threats included overexploitation, illicit grazing, mining and poaching.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The range forms one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s major watersheds, whose rivers drain almost 40 per cent of the country's land area, among them the Godavari, Kaveri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. Most flow east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, and many are dammed for irrigation and hydroelectric power. The mountains block the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> winds from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>, bringing heavy rain to the west coast and leaving a rain shadow over the interior of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>.</p><p>The Western Ghats are a biodiversity hotspot that holds nearly 30 per cent of the species of flora and fauna found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, most of them endemic, and at least 325 globally threatened species. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a> has established protected areas, including two biosphere reserves and 13 national parks, and the Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve is the largest contiguous protected area. In 2012 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> declared 39 sites in seven clusters across the range World Heritage Sites.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Western Ghats” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Hoysalas were hill chiefs of the Malnad, the forested uplands of the <b>Western Ghats</b> in southern Karnataka.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The founder, Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj (1630–1680), son of the Bijapur commander Shahaji Bhonsle, used the rugged terrain of the <b>Western Ghats</b> and his grasp of guerrilla warfare to seize strategically placed forts such as Torna, Rajgad and later Raigad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Passes through the <b>Western Ghats</b>, such as the Naneghat, linked inland towns like Pratishthana, the modern Paithan, and Tagara, the modern Ter, with ports at Kalyan and Sopara, while the eastern territories opened on to the Bay of Bengal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Alupas ruled the coastal strip of Karnataka between the <b>Western Ghats</b> and the Arabian Sea.</blockquote><a href="/state/alupa-dynasty">Alupa dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>The chiefs governed a hilly, forested territory on the <b>Ghats</b> overlooking the Konkan, under Kolhapur overlordship and British supervision through the Kolhapur residency.</blockquote><a href="/state/bavda-state">Bavda State</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pant Sachivs ruled a hilly territory of forts and small farms along the <b>Western Ghats</b>, including Rohida and Rajgad.</blockquote><a href="/state/bhor-state">Bhor State</a></li></ul></section>
